1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:06,430
This is the second part of our discussion regarding the approach to interpretation of a head CT.

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:17,680
The overall learning objective for these modules will be that the learner should be able to evaluate a head CT using a systematic approach

3
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:23,920
and state the five key features that you need to assess when you're interpreting a head CT.

4
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:30,180
This module in general will deal with the concepts of vasogenic versus cytotoxic edema,

5
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:33,840
what they are and why it's important to differentiate these types of edema,

6
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:38,520
and then finally on identification of brain swelling and herniation.

7
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:44,840
Let's revisit the overall search pattern before we get into the details.

8
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:51,480
In part one, we talked specifically about assessment of the ventricular system and extra axial spaces,

9
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:54,960
as well as the identification of acute hemorrhage.

10
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:00,620
In this unit, we're going to talk in more depth about the assessment of the brain parenchyma,

11
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:04,580
specifically how do you assess for mass or mass effect.

12
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:07,320
We're going to talk about gray-white matter differentiation.

13
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:13,940
And then finally, this is a head CT, so we have to be complete in our assessment of all structures

14
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:18,080
and not just be concerned about the brain parenchyma itself,

15
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:22,340
so that we're going to review the findings that we may identify in the bones,

16
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,600
the paranasal sinuses and mastoid air cells, as well as the orbits and other soft tissues.

17
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:37,030
When a mass is identified on a head CT, there are two key questions that you have to answer.

18
00:01:37,570 --> 00:01:40,710
Number one, what's the cause of the mass? What's the etiology?

19
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:44,190
And number two are, what are the sequelae of the mass?

20
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:46,970
What is it doing to the adjacent parenchyma?

21
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:53,410
Is it just local mass effect, or is it causing more distant mass effect with herniation?

22
00:01:53,410 --> 00:01:59,990
Again, these are two examples of abnormal masses that are associated with mass effect.

23
00:02:00,210 --> 00:02:07,630
On the case on the left, we have an intraaxial high attenuation mass consistent with an intraprachymal hematoma.

24
00:02:08,590 --> 00:02:12,650
Notice that there is local mass effect with edema in the white matter.

25
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:19,270
You can identify this edema by the lower attenuation of the white matter on the left

26
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:22,510
compared to the patient's normal white matter on the right.

27
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:25,170
And also note the sulcal effacement.

28
00:02:25,650 --> 00:02:28,770
Notice that adjacent to the mass, you don't really see any of the sulci,

29
00:02:29,070 --> 00:02:34,790
and this is because the brain is swelling and effacing the sulci.

30
00:02:34,790 --> 00:02:41,890
On the case on the right, you don't really identify a distinct mass in and of itself,

31
00:02:42,010 --> 00:02:48,770
but we do see mass effect in the form of low attenuation and loss of the gray-white differentiation.

32
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:58,290
In this example of a right MCA infarct, where you can see asymmetric sulcal effacement.

33
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:04,770
Another term that you may hear of when talking about mass effect is midline shift

34
00:03:04,770 --> 00:03:06,490
or midline displacement of structures.

35
00:03:07,570 --> 00:03:11,510
So masses can cause a displacement of structures in the paramidline

36
00:03:11,510 --> 00:03:16,230
actually to move across the midline to the contralateral side.

37
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:21,050
So in this example, we have a large right-sided subdural hematoma

38
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:25,270
that results in displacement of the right-sided paramedian structures

39
00:03:25,270 --> 00:03:28,390
or paramidline structures, moving them to the left.

40
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:33,570
So in this example, if we were to draw an imaginary line in the midline

41
00:03:33,570 --> 00:03:35,530
at approximately the length of the falx,

42
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:40,350
we can see that there is actually a shift from that midline point

43
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:43,410
to, let's say, where the cavum septum pellucidum is,

44
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:46,690
which represents the true midline structure,

45
00:03:46,810 --> 00:03:52,210
we can see that there has been herniation of the right-sided brain structures

46
00:03:52,210 --> 00:03:55,070
under the falx to the left side.

47
00:03:55,170 --> 00:03:58,990
This is termed sub-falcine herniation, and we'll talk about this shortly.

48
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:05,180
But this is an example of midline shift or midline displacement of structures.

49
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:11,320
Let's move on now to assessment of the parenchyma in terms of its gray-white matter

50
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:11,940
differentiation.

51
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,020
It's normal to have this distinction.

52
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:23,600
The white matter, as we see here, is lower in attenuation or hypoattenuating relative

53
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:25,200
to the gray matter.

54
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:30,120
In this case, we're comparing the subcortical right-frontal white matter to the overlying

55
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:32,200
right-frontal cerebral cortex.

56
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:34,960
So this is a normal finding to have this distinction.

57
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:41,070
However, sometimes this distinction can be difficult to appreciate on CT,

58
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:46,130
given the limited contrast resolution of CT as compared with MRI.

59
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:52,610
So then the question becomes, is there anything that we can do to accentuate this gray-white

60
00:04:52,610 --> 00:04:55,570
matter differentiation on CT?

61
00:04:55,570 --> 00:05:01,690
And sometimes you can adjust your window, you can narrow your window width to help accentuate

62
00:05:01,690 --> 00:05:06,130
the subtle differences in attenuation between the gray matter and the white matter,

63
00:05:06,590 --> 00:05:08,170
the so-called stroke window.

64
00:05:09,010 --> 00:05:14,390
Now, its name implies that you would simply be using this to look for strokes alone, but

65
00:05:14,390 --> 00:05:20,310
in routine clinical practice, this may help you to identify other abnormalities of the

66
00:05:20,310 --> 00:05:22,530
parenchyma, such as subtle masses.

67
00:05:22,530 --> 00:05:30,710
The key here is just to be aware of this window, and when you're assessing the gray-white matter

68
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:35,870
distinction, to use this window to help you identify subtle abnormalities that may be present.

69
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:43,190
The question then becomes, what causes loss of the normal gray-white differentiation?

70
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:47,490
When you see this, you should be thinking of ischemia and infarction.

71
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:57,670
On the case on the left, take a second to compare side to side, particularly in the insular cortex,

72
00:05:58,670 --> 00:06:04,990
and look to see that there is subtle decrease or loss of the gray-white matter differentiation

73
00:06:04,990 --> 00:06:10,470
in the right insular cortex there, deep in the right sylvean fissure.

74
00:06:11,090 --> 00:06:15,810
Notice how you can't make out the overlying cortex from the underlying white matter.

75
00:06:15,810 --> 00:06:21,590
So the gray-white matter differentiation is lost, and this is an example of ischemia in

76
00:06:21,590 --> 00:06:24,790
a vascular territory, in this case, the right MCA.

77
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:27,590
So we're talking a focal area in the brain that's ischemic.

78
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:32,170
Ischemia can also be global, as we see in the case on the left.

79
00:06:32,650 --> 00:06:38,570
There's diffuse bilateral loss of gray-white matter differentiation in a patient with global

80
00:06:38,570 --> 00:06:40,390
hypoxic ischemic injury.

81
00:06:40,390 --> 00:06:49,330
So when you see loss of gray-white matter differentiation, think ischemia and infarction, and then be thinking

82
00:06:49,330 --> 00:06:53,170
what's the vascular territory, or is this a global abnormality?

83
00:06:55,310 --> 00:07:01,730
Diffuse cerebral edema will manifest itself as a decreased attenuation, resulting in loss

84
00:07:01,730 --> 00:07:03,210
of gray-white matter differentiation.

85
00:07:03,210 --> 00:07:09,290
And you will also be able to identify mass effect, as evidenced by effacement of the cerebral

86
00:07:09,290 --> 00:07:14,290
sulci, which we've seen examples of, as well as effacement of the basal cisterns, which

87
00:07:14,290 --> 00:07:15,310
we'll talk about shortly.

88
00:07:16,190 --> 00:07:19,850
Mass effect may also cause small or compressed ventricles.

89
00:07:21,470 --> 00:07:25,730
So the key things are diminished attenuation with loss of gray-white matter differentiation

90
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:27,750
and signs of mass effect.

91
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:36,450
The diffuse low attenuation or decreased attenuation of the brain parenchyma does not equally affect

92
00:07:36,450 --> 00:07:40,310
different types of tissue or the different portions of the brain.

93
00:07:40,770 --> 00:07:46,410
This can lead to what's called the reversal sign, where the thalamus, brainstem, and cerebellum

94
00:07:46,410 --> 00:07:53,070
retain their normal density in comparison to the more affected cerebral cortex, which becomes

95
00:07:53,070 --> 00:07:53,470
darker.

96
00:07:53,470 --> 00:07:59,550
So in this case, you can see how the cerebellum is higher in attenuation than the background

97
00:07:59,550 --> 00:08:04,710
cerebrum, the so-called white cerebellar sign of diffuse cerebral edema.

98
00:08:05,190 --> 00:08:12,450
These examples nicely lead into the discussion of cytotoxic versus vasogenic edema.

99
00:08:12,950 --> 00:08:16,570
These are the two types of edema patterns that we can identify on CT.

100
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:23,690
Distinguishing cytotoxic versus vasogenic edema is important because it can help suggest the

101
00:08:23,690 --> 00:08:24,290
etiology.

102
00:08:24,470 --> 00:08:28,430
That is, it can help us narrow the differential diagnosis for the cause of edema.

103
00:08:29,670 --> 00:08:32,870
Cytotoxic edema refers to cellular swelling.

104
00:08:33,690 --> 00:08:40,110
It's seen in the presence of ischemia, where the sodium-potassium pump on the cell membranes

105
00:08:40,110 --> 00:08:41,570
is unable to function.

106
00:08:42,210 --> 00:08:48,310
Water moves into the cell, and you get cellular swelling, which manifests itself as loss or

107
00:08:48,310 --> 00:08:51,290
decrease in the gray-white matter distinction.

108
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:59,370
As we can see in this example of ischemia in the right middle cerebral artery territory, and

109
00:08:59,370 --> 00:09:05,990
we can see the loss of the gray-white matter differentiation in the distribution of the MCA.

110
00:09:08,370 --> 00:09:11,750
Let's contrast cytotoxic edema with vasogenic edema.

111
00:09:12,450 --> 00:09:15,650
Now, vasogenic edema is caused by leaky capillaries.

112
00:09:15,650 --> 00:09:18,450
So there's an abnormal blood-brain barrier.

113
00:09:19,170 --> 00:09:23,350
This is often associated with neoplasm or abscess and not ischemia.

114
00:09:24,450 --> 00:09:25,790
The gray matter is spared.

115
00:09:26,490 --> 00:09:31,690
And actually, the gray-white matter differentiation is typically accentuated because there's sparing

116
00:09:31,690 --> 00:09:37,570
of the gray matter, but the white matter becomes even lower in attenuation because of the edema,

117
00:09:37,570 --> 00:09:42,530
so that the difference in attenuation between the gray and white matter actually increases.

118
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:51,090
In this example, we see abnormality in the left frontal white matter with sparing of the

119
00:09:51,090 --> 00:09:52,890
overlying cortex, as we see here.

120
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:59,150
This should make us think of neoplasm and abscess, even though we don't directly see the mass

121
00:09:59,150 --> 00:10:02,710
itself, but this would not be caused by ischemia.

122
00:10:03,870 --> 00:10:08,750
So to summarize, we want to think of cytotoxic edema, loss of gray-white matter differentiation,

123
00:10:09,110 --> 00:10:17,370
ischemia infarct, vasogenic edema, leaky capillaries, neoplasm, and abscess as examples of etiologies

124
00:10:17,370 --> 00:10:19,070
that might cause vasogenic edema.

125
00:10:19,070 --> 00:10:25,230
So let's look at the MRI from that patient that we just saw on the prior slide.

126
00:10:26,130 --> 00:10:32,150
The left-sided image shows a T2-weighted sequence with fat saturation, showing extensive edema

127
00:10:32,150 --> 00:10:35,350
in the left frontal white matter.

128
00:10:35,870 --> 00:10:40,910
Remember, T2-weighted sequences show fluid as being high signal intensity, so we have high

129
00:10:40,910 --> 00:10:45,570
signal intensity in the white matter with sparing of the overlying cortex.

130
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:52,070
On the T1-weighted image, because fluid is low in signal relative to the gray matter,

131
00:10:52,610 --> 00:10:58,230
we can see low signal intensity there corresponding to the high signal intensity on the T2-weighted

132
00:10:58,230 --> 00:10:58,590
sequence.

133
00:10:59,290 --> 00:11:05,550
And then finally, on the right, we have a post-contrast T1-weighted sequence where we can actually see

134
00:11:05,550 --> 00:11:07,150
the enhancing mass.

135
00:11:07,150 --> 00:11:13,690
So even though on the CT, we didn't see the mass itself, because of the pattern of edema,

136
00:11:13,830 --> 00:11:19,390
we were going to be favoring abscess or neoplasm as opposed to ischemia.

137
00:11:19,890 --> 00:11:25,040
This is an example of an intracranial metastasis.

138
00:11:32,670 --> 00:11:37,610
This question is testing your ability to distinguish cytotoxic from vasogenic edema.

139
00:11:37,610 --> 00:11:44,410
Remember, cytotoxic edema, cellular swelling, ischemia, the gray matter, and white matter

140
00:11:44,410 --> 00:11:45,770
differentiation is lost.

141
00:11:46,250 --> 00:11:52,950
With vasogenic edema, it's leaky capillaries, typically from abscess or neoplasm, and the

142
00:11:52,950 --> 00:11:57,790
gray-white matter differentiation is maintained because there's sparing of the gray matter.

143
00:11:58,610 --> 00:12:04,270
In this example, there's actually bilateral low attenuation in the white matter.

144
00:12:04,270 --> 00:12:11,210
As an example, in the left frontal lobe, we have decreased attenuation in the left frontal

145
00:12:11,210 --> 00:12:16,630
white matter with the yellow arrow, but notice that the cortex, as identified on the blue

146
00:12:16,630 --> 00:12:17,570
arrow, is spared.

147
00:12:18,270 --> 00:12:20,890
So this would be an example of vasogenic edema.

148
00:12:21,450 --> 00:12:24,130
In this case, the etiology was a metastasis.

149
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:30,030
We need to now turn our attention to evaluation of the basal cisterns.

150
00:12:30,730 --> 00:12:35,450
Remember that the basal cisterns are in large subarachnoid spaces, so they contain

151
00:12:35,450 --> 00:12:36,610
cerebrospinal fluid.

152
00:12:37,590 --> 00:12:44,690
The image on the left shows the supercellar cistern containing the circle of Willis vessels

153
00:12:44,690 --> 00:12:45,850
as well as CSF.

154
00:12:46,990 --> 00:12:51,610
In the example on the right, we have that quadrigeminal plate cistern that looks like a smile.

155
00:12:52,690 --> 00:12:56,230
So these cisterns, we need to evaluate for their attenuation.

156
00:12:56,230 --> 00:13:02,670
Remember, they should be simple fluid attenuation of CSF, and we need to evaluate their patency.

157
00:13:03,510 --> 00:13:06,470
Abnormal attenuation may be a sign of underlying hemorrhage.

158
00:13:06,970 --> 00:13:13,710
Abnormal appearance in terms of symmetry or loss of patency is a sign of herniation or

159
00:13:13,710 --> 00:13:14,430
brain swelling.

160
00:13:16,650 --> 00:13:21,730
When evaluating for attenuation of the basal cisterns, we are essentially looking for blood.

161
00:13:21,730 --> 00:13:27,690
That is, acute blood will be high in attenuation and will replace the normal fluid attenuation

162
00:13:27,690 --> 00:13:29,430
within the basal cisterns.

163
00:13:29,870 --> 00:13:36,030
In this example, we have high attenuation hemorrhage replacing the normal CSF in the supercellar

164
00:13:36,030 --> 00:13:36,570
cistern.

165
00:13:37,270 --> 00:13:40,230
So therefore, this is subarachnoid in location.

166
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:47,500
The second thing we need to evaluate for is the patency of the basal cisterns.

167
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:52,760
That is, are the basal cisterns open and are they normal morphology and symmetric?

168
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:59,660
What we are really assessing for here is any brain swelling or herniation that may be causing

169
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:01,960
effacement or asymmetry of the cisterns.

170
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:08,580
On the case on the left, we have a normal example showing a widely patent supercellar

171
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:11,740
cistern and quadrigeminal plate cistern.

172
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:18,320
Compare this to the case on the right where there is diffuse brain swelling, diffuse loss

173
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:24,380
of gray-white matter differentiation, but notice that there is a very slit-like supercellar

174
00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:29,640
cistern and essentially complete effacement of the quadrigeminal plate cisterns.

175
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:36,120
This is from diffuse swelling of the brain causing narrowing or effacement of the cisterns.

176
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:44,190
We will begin our discussion of brain herniations using this coronal illustration as an overview

177
00:14:44,190 --> 00:14:46,770
of the different types of herniations you may encounter.

178
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:53,170
The first thing to be aware of is that a brain herniation represents an emergent finding

179
00:14:53,170 --> 00:14:56,110
given its potential lethal consequences.

180
00:14:57,350 --> 00:15:01,210
These findings must be rapidly communicated with the clinical service.

181
00:15:02,330 --> 00:15:06,570
A herniation refers to a protrusion of brain parenchyma through a defect or opening.

182
00:15:06,570 --> 00:15:16,390
The most common herniation refers to herniation of the cingulate gyrus beneath the level of

183
00:15:16,390 --> 00:15:21,710
the phalx, the so-called subphalcine herniation or midline shift of structures.

184
00:15:23,050 --> 00:15:31,590
A central or transtentorial herniation refers to the downward shift of medially located brain

185
00:15:31,590 --> 00:15:34,210
structures through the tentorial notch.

186
00:15:34,210 --> 00:15:40,250
Remember, the tentorial notch or incisora is an opening in the tentorium through which the

187
00:15:40,250 --> 00:15:41,510
brain stem extends.

188
00:15:43,910 --> 00:15:50,410
Uncle herniation refers to herniation of the medial portion of the temporal lobe, the

189
00:15:50,410 --> 00:15:55,870
uncas, over the tentorium and into the supercellar cistern.

190
00:15:58,070 --> 00:16:03,490
Posterior fossa masses may result in upward cerebellar herniation.

191
00:16:03,490 --> 00:16:13,370
That is, herniation of the cerebellar tissues up through the tentorium going into the supertentorial

192
00:16:13,370 --> 00:16:13,950
compartment.

193
00:16:14,710 --> 00:16:18,250
This is upward cerebellar transtentorial herniation.

194
00:16:19,330 --> 00:16:27,030
Alternatively, a posterior fossa mass may also cause cerebellar tissues to herniate downward

195
00:16:27,030 --> 00:16:33,710
through the foramen magnum, the so-called downward cerebellar herniation or tonsillar herniation

196
00:16:33,710 --> 00:16:38,070
as the cerebellar tonsils descend through the foramen magnum.

197
00:16:39,190 --> 00:16:46,250
Finally, we have a transcalvarial herniation in which brain tissue herniates through a defect

198
00:16:46,250 --> 00:16:47,990
in the overlying calvarium.

199
00:16:47,990 --> 00:16:53,690
The subfalcine herniation is the most common herniation pattern.

200
00:16:54,590 --> 00:17:00,630
It refers to displacement of the brain, typically the cingulate gyrus, beneath the free edge of

201
00:17:00,630 --> 00:17:01,210
the falks.

202
00:17:02,490 --> 00:17:08,370
In this case, we have a coronal T2-weighted MRI in an 82-year-old gentleman that fell and

203
00:17:08,370 --> 00:17:10,650
had a subdural hematoma on the left.

204
00:17:11,830 --> 00:17:16,410
Remember, when we identify a mass, we want to identify the etiology of the mass and then

205
00:17:16,410 --> 00:17:17,910
the associated mass effect.

206
00:17:17,910 --> 00:17:21,690
If we look at the midline here, we have the falks.

207
00:17:22,130 --> 00:17:28,330
We'll notice that that subdural hematoma is pushing the left-sided cingulate gyrus underneath

208
00:17:28,330 --> 00:17:32,510
that falks or subfalcine herniation.

209
00:17:33,290 --> 00:17:41,950
Associated complications with subfalcine herniation include ACA or anterior cerebral artery infarcts

210
00:17:41,950 --> 00:17:47,890
as well as contralateral hydrocephalus that you can get from obstruction of the foramen of

211
00:17:47,890 --> 00:17:48,890
Monroe.

212
00:17:48,890 --> 00:17:56,730
Both of these complications are caused by the mass effect associated with dragging that cingulate

213
00:17:56,730 --> 00:17:59,830
gyrus and associated brain structures across the midline.

214
00:18:01,950 --> 00:18:06,550
Because we're primarily concerned with CT imaging in this module, we need to understand

215
00:18:06,550 --> 00:18:09,470
what subfalcine herniation looks like on axial images.

216
00:18:10,330 --> 00:18:15,810
The image on the left is the image from the previous slide showing the coronal view where

217
00:18:15,810 --> 00:18:20,950
you have that cingulate gyrus on the left herniating beneath the free edge of the falks.

218
00:18:20,950 --> 00:18:30,070
On the axial view on the right here, another T2 weighted sequence, we can see if we draw

219
00:18:30,070 --> 00:18:36,230
a line at the midline where the falks is, we can see that the left-sided cingulate gyrus

220
00:18:36,230 --> 00:18:40,250
is herniated beneath the falks and across the midline.

221
00:18:40,250 --> 00:18:47,570
You can also see the flow voids in the midline that are the anterior cerebellar arteries, and

222
00:18:47,570 --> 00:18:53,270
given this location, that's why you can have infarction of the ACA territory, typically on

223
00:18:53,270 --> 00:18:56,250
the ipsilateral side with this type of herniation.

224
00:18:56,250 --> 00:19:03,470
Finally, we can quantify the degree of displacement by finding a midline structure, typically the

225
00:19:03,470 --> 00:19:08,510
cavum septum pellucidum, or in this case, we can use kind of the midline between the two

226
00:19:08,510 --> 00:19:15,470
anterior cerebral arteries, and we can measure from that point to the imaginary midline that

227
00:19:15,470 --> 00:19:22,290
we made along the expected position of the falks to actually quantify the degree of midline

228
00:19:22,290 --> 00:19:22,790
shift.

229
00:19:22,790 --> 00:19:32,000
Uncoherniation refers to herniation of the mesial temporal lobe, or the uncus, across

230
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:37,660
the tentorial notch, or that is, across the free edge of the tentorium into the supercellular

231
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:38,240
cistern.

232
00:19:38,660 --> 00:19:44,000
This type of herniation can be associated with mass effect on the supercellular cistern as

233
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:45,480
well as the ambient cistern.

234
00:19:46,040 --> 00:19:53,300
It can result in compression of the brainstem as well as PCA infarction because the posterior

235
00:19:53,300 --> 00:20:01,200
cerebral artery runs in the ambient cistern, which can be compressed by the uncoherniation.

236
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:06,200
In this example, I'm showing a coronal reconstructed view from a CT.

237
00:20:06,200 --> 00:20:13,200
There's a large left-sided intraparenchymal hematoma that's resulting in significant mass effect.

238
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:18,860
As we can see, the ipsilateral, or the left-sided in this case, mesial part of the temporal lobe,

239
00:20:18,940 --> 00:20:25,300
or the uncus, is pushed across the tentorium into the midline and into the supercellular

240
00:20:25,300 --> 00:20:25,840
cistern.

241
00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:33,200
Again, because we're talking about CT and the vast majority of your interpretation on CT is

242
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:37,560
going to be in the axial plane, we need to understand what this looks like on an axial

243
00:20:37,560 --> 00:20:37,900
image.

244
00:20:37,900 --> 00:20:42,780
So we're talking about the mesial, or the midline portion of the temporal lobe, which

245
00:20:42,780 --> 00:20:49,400
is the uncus sliding across the tentorium and facing the supercellular cistern.

246
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:53,320
So take a second to try to find that on this axial image.

247
00:20:54,660 --> 00:20:56,980
Identify where the supercellular cistern is.

248
00:20:59,490 --> 00:21:06,110
Hopefully you saw the right-sided uncus that's herniated out across the tentorium, causing asymmetry

249
00:21:06,110 --> 00:21:08,930
in the appearance of the supercellular cistern.

250
00:21:11,540 --> 00:21:18,020
Posterior fossile masses can result in either upward cerebellar herniation, that is the cerebellum

251
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:25,700
herniates up through the tentorium and into the supertentorial compartment, or downward tonsillar

252
00:21:25,700 --> 00:21:30,900
herniation, in which the cerebellar tonsils herniate below the level of the foramen magnum.

253
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:35,480
This is a sagittal T1 view of a patient with medulloblastoma.

254
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:41,580
We can see signal abnormality within the cerebellum here.

255
00:21:42,980 --> 00:21:47,780
Notice that this person actually has both upward and downward cerebellar tonsillar herniation.

256
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:51,960
But on this slide, we're going to concentrate on the tonsillar herniation.

257
00:21:52,620 --> 00:21:59,320
Notice how the cerebellar tonsils, as shown by the arrow, are pushed downward by the mass

258
00:21:59,320 --> 00:22:01,660
effect from the medulloblastoma.

259
00:22:02,180 --> 00:22:09,240
So the tonsils are descended, they descend inferiorly below the level of the foramen magnum.

260
00:22:09,480 --> 00:22:15,080
Now this can cause compression, if bad enough, of the brainstem against the clibus.

261
00:22:15,800 --> 00:22:21,320
There is also non-life-threatening tonsillar descent in the spectrum of Chiari malformations,

262
00:22:21,320 --> 00:22:24,060
which aren't true herniations, per se.

263
00:22:26,680 --> 00:22:29,100
This is the same patient with medulloblastoma.

264
00:22:29,540 --> 00:22:34,220
This time we're going to concentrate on the upward herniation of the cerebellum.

265
00:22:34,580 --> 00:22:41,280
That is, the cerebellum is pushed up through the tentorium and into the supertentorial compartment.

266
00:22:42,180 --> 00:22:48,120
As we can see by the arrow, there is obliteration of the superior cerebellar cistern

267
00:22:48,120 --> 00:22:54,260
by the cerebellar tissue that's being pushed upwards from the posterior fossa mass.

268
00:22:56,650 --> 00:23:02,070
I'm beginning discussion of these cerebellar herniations using sagittal views

269
00:23:02,070 --> 00:23:04,210
because I believe it's easier to understand this.

270
00:23:04,630 --> 00:23:07,910
Ultimately, we're going to have to understand, especially when we're interpreting head CT,

271
00:23:08,350 --> 00:23:10,410
what these herniations look like on axials.

272
00:23:10,630 --> 00:23:17,090
But I just want to take a second here to reinforce what these cerebellar herniations look like

273
00:23:17,090 --> 00:23:24,050
by comparing a normal sagittal T1 on the left to the medulloblastoma case that we've been looking at on the right.

274
00:23:25,270 --> 00:23:31,890
Notice the normal CSF space along the cranial margin of the cerebellum, the superior cerebellar cistern.

275
00:23:32,390 --> 00:23:38,970
In this case of upward cerebellar herniation where the cerebellum is pushed up through the tentorium

276
00:23:38,970 --> 00:23:44,070
into the supertentorial compartment, that superior cerebellar cistern is obliterated

277
00:23:44,070 --> 00:23:48,330
as the CSF is pushed away by that herniated cerebellar tissue.

278
00:23:50,030 --> 00:23:54,250
In the normal case, we can see the cerebellar tonsils above the level of the foramen magnum,

279
00:23:55,830 --> 00:24:00,150
whereas with the posterior fossa mass, the cerebellar tonsils are pushed downwards

280
00:24:00,150 --> 00:24:05,050
through the foramen magnum lying along the posterior aspect of the junction

281
00:24:05,050 --> 00:24:10,450
between the medulla of the brainstem and the cervical spinal cord.

282
00:24:10,450 --> 00:24:16,670
Since we're concentrating on head CT interpretation in this module,

283
00:24:16,930 --> 00:24:22,930
we really need to translate this knowledge of cerebellar herniation to the axial images.

284
00:24:23,050 --> 00:24:25,590
So what do these herniations look like on axial views?

285
00:24:26,390 --> 00:24:32,110
In the case on the far left, we have a normal case where you have a cervical medullary junction

286
00:24:32,110 --> 00:24:38,670
here at the foramen magnum and a nice CSF space around that junction.

287
00:24:38,670 --> 00:24:40,070
So that's a normal case.

288
00:24:40,550 --> 00:24:47,490
In the middle image, we have the same patient seven hours later in a case with diffuse cerebral edema,

289
00:24:47,910 --> 00:24:54,250
causing mass effect and pushing the cerebellar tonsils inferiorly below the foramen magnum.

290
00:24:54,690 --> 00:24:57,410
So we're going to have the cervical medullary junction here.

291
00:24:57,890 --> 00:25:01,370
But notice now how that CSF space is obliterated,

292
00:25:01,370 --> 00:25:08,390
where that fluid attenuation of the cerebrospinal fluid is now replaced by the cerebellar tonsils,

293
00:25:08,550 --> 00:25:09,990
which are pushed inferiorly.

294
00:25:10,670 --> 00:25:14,090
So when scrolling through a set of axial images, you really have to be cognizant of,

295
00:25:14,250 --> 00:25:16,070
okay, where is the foramen magnum?

296
00:25:16,930 --> 00:25:19,010
And then as I go caudal to this,

297
00:25:19,510 --> 00:25:23,950
am I seeing cerebellar tissue or am I seeing the normal CSF that I should be seeing?

298
00:25:23,950 --> 00:25:29,110
The image on the right is from a different case, but again shows tonsillar herniation.

299
00:25:29,990 --> 00:25:33,090
We have the cervical medullary junction with the asterisks.

300
00:25:33,890 --> 00:25:40,810
And then notice how that CSF space behind that junction is gone and actually replaced

301
00:25:40,810 --> 00:25:44,870
by soft tissue attenuation of the cerebellar tonsils.

302
00:25:44,870 --> 00:25:51,060
With upward or ascending transtentorial herniation,

303
00:25:52,020 --> 00:25:59,500
a posterior fossa mass or mass effect causes the cerebellar tissue to herniate upwards

304
00:25:59,500 --> 00:26:02,740
through the tentorial incisor or notch.

305
00:26:03,180 --> 00:26:07,260
Remember, the tentorium has an opening through which the brainstem extends,

306
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:09,280
so this is the tentorial notch.

307
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:16,000
On the coronal post-contrast T1-weighted sequence on the left,

308
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:22,100
we can see the tentorium and we can see the opening in the tentorium as we go towards the midline.

309
00:26:22,740 --> 00:26:29,400
Here with upward herniation, we see cerebellar tissue herniating upward through that notch

310
00:26:29,400 --> 00:26:32,000
or opening into the supratentorial compartment.

311
00:26:32,680 --> 00:26:36,460
This can be a little more difficult to identify on axial imaging,

312
00:26:36,460 --> 00:26:42,800
but we can find the tentorium here and we see in the midline we have some of that cerebellar tissue

313
00:26:42,800 --> 00:26:49,980
that is actually going to be extending cranial to that notch up into the supratentorial compartment.

314
00:26:50,160 --> 00:26:55,460
So you get a better sense of this on a set of axial images where you are scrolling back and forth

315
00:26:55,460 --> 00:26:58,240
to find where the opening is in the tentorium

316
00:26:58,240 --> 00:27:04,200
and identifying that the cerebellar tissue extends above that opening.

317
00:27:04,200 --> 00:27:17,150
In this case, test your ability to identify a subfalcine herniation or midline shift.

318
00:27:17,850 --> 00:27:22,270
Here we have an extra-axial hematoma as indicated by the red asterix.

319
00:27:23,170 --> 00:27:26,270
The chrysanthic shape is indicative of a subdural location.

320
00:27:27,330 --> 00:27:30,470
Remember that when you identify an intracranial mass,

321
00:27:30,570 --> 00:27:34,470
it's important to not only think about the etiology of the mass, that is, what is the mass,

322
00:27:34,470 --> 00:27:39,550
but also the effect of the mass and what it's having on the adjacent brain parenchyma.

323
00:27:40,430 --> 00:27:44,230
In this case, there's local mass effect with salsal effacement.

324
00:27:44,470 --> 00:27:49,670
Notice that you don't see any salsi deep to the subdural hematoma.

325
00:27:50,170 --> 00:27:53,710
Compare that to the right side where you see the nice, normal salsi.

326
00:27:54,810 --> 00:27:59,350
Also notice, however, that there is midline displacement of that cingulate gyrus

327
00:27:59,350 --> 00:28:03,130
underneath the falcs as indicated by the yellow arrow.

328
00:28:03,630 --> 00:28:06,270
The blue arrows show you the normal midline falcs.

329
00:28:06,630 --> 00:28:11,070
You can see that there is brain tissue herniating from left to right across this.

330
00:28:11,890 --> 00:28:14,670
This is consistent with subfalcine herniation.

331
00:28:16,450 --> 00:28:21,350
Now, the tendency in reading a head CT is to concentrate only on the brain parenchyma

332
00:28:21,890 --> 00:28:23,710
and intracranial contents itself.

333
00:28:23,710 --> 00:28:27,950
However, to be thorough, we need to look at everything on the image.

334
00:28:28,470 --> 00:28:32,150
This will include the bones, orbits, soft tissues, etc.

335
00:28:33,010 --> 00:28:38,290
So we're going to now go into sort of the stuff outside the brain substance itself.

336
00:28:38,730 --> 00:28:41,090
We're going to start with the bones, and we're going to, again,

337
00:28:41,210 --> 00:28:43,430
optimize our window and algorithm setting.

338
00:28:43,530 --> 00:28:46,170
So we're going to look on the bone algorithm, bone windows.

339
00:28:46,670 --> 00:28:49,990
This is going to allow us to assess the details of the bones

340
00:28:49,990 --> 00:28:57,110
that might not otherwise be obvious with other windows or reconstruction algorithms.

341
00:28:57,970 --> 00:29:00,610
Remember, abnormalities can be focal or diffuse.

342
00:29:01,490 --> 00:29:05,030
We're looking for aggressive things, especially when I use the term aggressive.

343
00:29:05,210 --> 00:29:06,850
I mean neoplasm or infection.

344
00:29:07,630 --> 00:29:10,130
In the setting of trauma, we're going to be looking for fractures.

345
00:29:10,850 --> 00:29:12,710
And remember, in all of these settings,

346
00:29:12,870 --> 00:29:14,990
especially if you're dealing with pediatric patients

347
00:29:14,990 --> 00:29:22,750
or if we're at the skull base and there's a lot of forainment and structures,

348
00:29:23,210 --> 00:29:24,710
symmetry is your friend, okay?

349
00:29:24,950 --> 00:29:26,830
We should be symmetric from right to left.

350
00:29:28,630 --> 00:29:31,110
So use this to your advantage.

351
00:29:33,590 --> 00:29:35,990
We're going to use bone windows and the bone algorithm

352
00:29:35,990 --> 00:29:39,730
to evaluate the bones of the calvarium and skull base.

353
00:29:39,730 --> 00:29:48,170
On the case in the left image, we see a lucent lesion in the left frontal bone

354
00:29:48,170 --> 00:29:52,710
involving the diploic space and extending into both cortices.

355
00:29:52,890 --> 00:29:57,270
You can see loss of the normal cortex, both on the inner and outer table of the skull.

356
00:29:58,490 --> 00:30:02,990
In the case on the right, notice the asymmetry and appearance of the sphenoid bone.

357
00:30:02,990 --> 00:30:08,370
Normal foramen on the right, but notice on the left side,

358
00:30:08,470 --> 00:30:11,490
we have absence of bone and neural foramina.

359
00:30:12,430 --> 00:30:16,490
That's an aggressive lesion, which turned out to be a metastasis.

360
00:30:17,510 --> 00:30:21,390
The point here is not that you'd be able to characterize all these bone abnormalities,

361
00:30:21,810 --> 00:30:24,350
but that you put this in your search pattern.

362
00:30:26,950 --> 00:30:32,050
One of the common indications for head CT is to assess for intracranial abnormalities

363
00:30:32,050 --> 00:30:35,570
in the setting of trauma, so we need to look carefully for fractures.

364
00:30:36,250 --> 00:30:43,930
One of the more difficult things early on is to distinguish sutures from calvarial fractures.

365
00:30:44,910 --> 00:30:47,990
Sutures have sclerotic margins and serpentine edges,

366
00:30:48,490 --> 00:30:54,330
whereas fractures tend to be more linear and straight and lack the sclerotic margins.

367
00:30:55,230 --> 00:30:59,050
Also, symmetry can be your friend depending on what types of sutures you're looking at.

368
00:30:59,050 --> 00:31:02,330
So, for instance, the coronal suture should be symmetric from side to side,

369
00:31:02,990 --> 00:31:05,370
whereas a fracture, you wouldn't expect symmetry.

370
00:31:06,230 --> 00:31:12,050
On the case on the left here, we have a not-so-subtle displaced fracture of the calvarium.

371
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:16,560
This case would not be a diagnostic dilemma.

372
00:31:17,340 --> 00:31:20,980
However, the case on the right shows a more subtle fracture at the skull base.

373
00:31:21,520 --> 00:31:26,220
Again, the skull base can be a very difficult area to identify

374
00:31:26,220 --> 00:31:27,600
because there's a lot of foramen.

375
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:30,440
The patient may not be perfectly centered in the gantry,

376
00:31:30,620 --> 00:31:35,200
so you may not have perfect right-to-left symmetry on a single axial cut.

377
00:31:36,720 --> 00:31:40,760
But there is a fracture in this case here in the left sphenoid bone.

378
00:31:43,260 --> 00:31:46,080
So, when assessing the bones, you want to look for focal abnormalities,

379
00:31:46,200 --> 00:31:50,600
whether aggressive things like infection or neoplasm or fractures.

380
00:31:50,600 --> 00:31:55,400
And then, finally, you want to be able to use these ancillary findings

381
00:31:55,400 --> 00:31:57,760
to maybe help you go back and assess the parenchyma.

382
00:31:57,880 --> 00:32:01,500
So, if you see a calvarial fracture,

383
00:32:01,660 --> 00:32:05,460
you may want to go take a second look at the intracranial contents

384
00:32:05,460 --> 00:32:09,420
to make sure you're not missing an extra-axial hematoma, for instance.

385
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:12,540
So, you can use these to help you identify

386
00:32:12,540 --> 00:32:16,980
what things you might want to look for more closely

387
00:32:16,980 --> 00:32:18,780
in the intracranial compartment.

388
00:32:18,780 --> 00:32:23,620
Once we've assessed the calvarium and skull base,

389
00:32:24,080 --> 00:32:27,320
we're going to keep the study on the bone algorithm and bone windows

390
00:32:27,320 --> 00:32:30,700
and assess the paranasal sinuses and mastoid air cells.

391
00:32:31,460 --> 00:32:33,040
These should be well aerated

392
00:32:33,040 --> 00:32:36,760
and have normal gas attenuation throughout.

393
00:32:37,240 --> 00:32:40,440
There should be no opacification with soft tissue or fluid,

394
00:32:40,780 --> 00:32:42,260
no fluid levels.

395
00:32:43,580 --> 00:32:46,820
There should be an intact osseous architecture

396
00:32:46,820 --> 00:32:51,020
to both the mastoid air cells as well as intact walls of the paranasal sinuses.

397
00:32:51,020 --> 00:32:55,440
And again, use symmetry as your friend comparing right to left.

398
00:32:55,440 --> 00:33:00,980
Let's look at some examples of abnormalities of the paranasal sinuses.

399
00:33:01,480 --> 00:33:03,980
On the image on the left,

400
00:33:04,240 --> 00:33:09,160
we have a fracture of the anterior wall of the right maxillary sinus.

401
00:33:09,960 --> 00:33:14,860
Notice that the normally aerated sinus has been partially replaced by fluid

402
00:33:14,860 --> 00:33:17,720
with the resultant gas fluid level.

403
00:33:17,720 --> 00:33:21,800
This is from a fracture of the maxillary sinus.

404
00:33:22,180 --> 00:33:27,680
In addition, this patient also has intracranial gas related to a separate fracture.

405
00:33:28,620 --> 00:33:30,020
In the image on the right,

406
00:33:30,300 --> 00:33:33,760
we'll notice that the ethmoid air cells on the left

407
00:33:33,760 --> 00:33:38,820
have been replaced by an osseous attenuation abnormality.

408
00:33:38,820 --> 00:33:41,700
This was a tumor of the ethmoid air cells.

409
00:33:42,500 --> 00:33:45,640
So again, we're assessing the paranasal sinuses.

410
00:33:45,800 --> 00:33:50,060
We want to make sure that they are normally aerated with intact walls.

411
00:33:52,610 --> 00:33:55,970
The mastoid air cells and middle ear cavity should be well aerated.

412
00:33:56,210 --> 00:33:58,170
That is, they should be of gas attenuation.

413
00:33:59,230 --> 00:34:02,310
This is a patient with left-sided ear pain.

414
00:34:03,270 --> 00:34:04,830
On the image on the left,

415
00:34:05,010 --> 00:34:09,270
which is the patient's right middle ear cavity and mastoid air cells,

416
00:34:09,270 --> 00:34:13,250
notice the normal aeration of the middle ear cavity

417
00:34:13,250 --> 00:34:18,270
and the mastoid air cells with intact osseous architecture.

418
00:34:18,910 --> 00:34:21,130
Compare that with the symptomatic side

419
00:34:21,130 --> 00:34:24,410
where you have replacement of the gas attenuation

420
00:34:24,410 --> 00:34:28,550
within the middle ear cavity by fluid attenuation material.

421
00:34:28,990 --> 00:34:31,570
Same thing in the mastoid air cells.

422
00:34:31,810 --> 00:34:35,350
These have been replaced by fluid attenuation material

423
00:34:35,350 --> 00:34:38,650
in a patient with left-sided mastoiditis

424
00:34:38,650 --> 00:34:41,650
or inflammation and infection of the mastoid air cells.

425
00:34:42,130 --> 00:34:46,840
We're going to end our evaluation of head CT

426
00:34:46,840 --> 00:34:49,280
with assessment of the orbits and soft tissues.

427
00:34:49,840 --> 00:34:52,320
For this, we're going to use soft tissue windows.

428
00:34:52,900 --> 00:34:55,640
We're going to assess the orbits for the globes themselves

429
00:34:55,640 --> 00:34:57,460
as well as the interorbital contents,

430
00:34:57,580 --> 00:34:59,860
including the optic nerve, muscles,

431
00:34:59,860 --> 00:35:01,660
and interorbital fat.

432
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:04,600
We're going to assess the soft tissues of the scalp,

433
00:35:04,960 --> 00:35:08,080
face, and image portions of the suprahyoid neck.

434
00:35:09,140 --> 00:35:12,720
And again, we want to look for symmetry from right to left.

435
00:35:15,080 --> 00:35:17,880
Just want to take a second to reiterate the fact that

436
00:35:17,880 --> 00:35:20,380
at this point, you should not be expected

437
00:35:20,380 --> 00:35:22,880
to identify all the causes

438
00:35:22,880 --> 00:35:25,140
or understand everything you're looking at

439
00:35:25,140 --> 00:35:26,420
in the orbits or soft tissues,

440
00:35:26,620 --> 00:35:29,560
like we talked about in the bones or paranasal sinuses.

441
00:35:29,560 --> 00:35:32,540
At this point, it's just a matter of putting this

442
00:35:32,540 --> 00:35:33,660
into your search pattern

443
00:35:33,660 --> 00:35:36,120
and identifying an abnormality.

444
00:35:36,700 --> 00:35:38,960
Characterizing that abnormality will come with time.

445
00:35:39,740 --> 00:35:41,100
The image on the left,

446
00:35:41,480 --> 00:35:44,020
if we assess for the orbits and interorbital contents,

447
00:35:44,560 --> 00:35:48,340
we can see a mass in the medial rectus muscle,

448
00:35:49,620 --> 00:35:51,340
whereas in the case on the right,

449
00:35:51,480 --> 00:35:52,940
in the suprahyoid neck,

450
00:35:53,640 --> 00:35:55,420
again, using symmetry as our friend,

451
00:35:55,800 --> 00:35:57,460
we can see an asymmetric mass

452
00:35:57,460 --> 00:35:59,140
in the right parotid gland.

453
00:35:59,560 --> 00:36:03,720
This case is a nice example

454
00:36:03,720 --> 00:36:05,740
of how all of this sort of fits together,

455
00:36:05,880 --> 00:36:07,060
where you have an abnormality

456
00:36:07,060 --> 00:36:08,580
in the intracranial compartment

457
00:36:08,580 --> 00:36:12,280
with associated findings in the soft tissues and bone.

458
00:36:12,840 --> 00:36:14,440
This is a case of trauma.

459
00:36:15,300 --> 00:36:17,860
Notice in assessment of the soft tissues

460
00:36:17,860 --> 00:36:21,260
that there is asymmetry in the appearance

461
00:36:21,260 --> 00:36:23,060
of the soft tissues of the scalp

462
00:36:23,060 --> 00:36:25,800
with swelling on the left side.

463
00:36:25,800 --> 00:36:30,660
Deep to this, we're going to have an extra-axial hematoma.

464
00:36:30,960 --> 00:36:34,460
Given its shape, we expect this to be epidural in location.

465
00:36:36,020 --> 00:36:37,580
And then on the bone windows,

466
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:42,260
we are able to see a non-displaced fracture

467
00:36:42,260 --> 00:36:43,680
associated with this.

468
00:36:44,380 --> 00:36:46,960
So in this case, the epidural hematoma is obvious,

469
00:36:46,960 --> 00:36:52,460
but sometimes it may be a subtle soft tissue swelling

470
00:36:52,460 --> 00:36:53,480
that you pick up

471
00:36:53,480 --> 00:36:57,420
or a subtle calvarial fracture that you detect

472
00:36:57,420 --> 00:37:00,120
that will cause you then to go scrutinize

473
00:37:00,120 --> 00:37:02,620
the intracranial compartment more carefully

474
00:37:02,620 --> 00:37:04,180
and may allow you to pick up

475
00:37:04,180 --> 00:37:08,020
a small extra-axial collection,

476
00:37:08,160 --> 00:37:09,540
in this example, a hematoma

477
00:37:09,540 --> 00:37:11,760
that you might not otherwise pick up.

478
00:37:11,760 --> 00:37:17,700
This completes our search pattern for a head CT.

479
00:37:18,080 --> 00:37:19,660
In the first module, we talked about

480
00:37:19,660 --> 00:37:21,560
assessment of the ventricular system

481
00:37:21,560 --> 00:37:23,360
and extra-axial spaces

482
00:37:23,360 --> 00:37:25,720
to get a sense of brain volume,

483
00:37:26,460 --> 00:37:28,160
mass effect on the ventricles,

484
00:37:28,240 --> 00:37:29,440
as well as hydrocephalus.

485
00:37:29,980 --> 00:37:32,560
We talked about different locations

486
00:37:32,560 --> 00:37:34,480
for acute intracranial hemorrhage,

487
00:37:34,860 --> 00:37:36,800
how acutely this is high in attenuation

488
00:37:36,800 --> 00:37:38,020
but evolves with time.

489
00:37:38,740 --> 00:37:41,400
We looked at the brain parenchyma itself

490
00:37:41,400 --> 00:37:43,500
for masses or mass effect

491
00:37:43,500 --> 00:37:45,800
as evidenced by sulcal effacement,

492
00:37:47,140 --> 00:37:49,400
abnormalities of the ventricular system

493
00:37:49,400 --> 00:37:50,340
as we talked about,

494
00:37:51,380 --> 00:37:53,020
effacement of the basal cisterns,

495
00:37:53,500 --> 00:37:55,220
and different types of brain herniation.

496
00:37:56,240 --> 00:37:58,520
We looked at assessment of the gray-white matter

497
00:37:58,520 --> 00:38:01,760
and the fact that there should be

498
00:38:01,760 --> 00:38:03,360
a differentiation between the two

499
00:38:03,360 --> 00:38:05,100
with the white matter being slightly lower

500
00:38:05,100 --> 00:38:06,560
in attenuation than the gray matter.

501
00:38:07,800 --> 00:38:10,600
We looked at the different types of edema,

502
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:12,780
both cytotoxic and vasogenic edema,

503
00:38:12,980 --> 00:38:14,540
and how this affects the gray-white matter

504
00:38:14,540 --> 00:38:15,140
differentiation.

505
00:38:16,740 --> 00:38:19,780
Then going outside of the brain parenchyma itself

506
00:38:19,780 --> 00:38:21,760
and intracranial contents,

507
00:38:22,200 --> 00:38:25,060
we talked about the bones,

508
00:38:25,560 --> 00:38:27,620
paranasal sinuses, and mastoid air cells,

509
00:38:27,700 --> 00:38:30,240
and assessing the orbits in soft tissue.

510
00:38:30,940 --> 00:38:33,920
We saw examples of how assessment

511
00:38:33,920 --> 00:38:35,660
of these structures outside

512
00:38:35,660 --> 00:38:37,220
the intracranial compartment

513
00:38:37,220 --> 00:38:38,780
may help us diagnose

514
00:38:38,780 --> 00:38:40,680
important intracranial findings

515
00:38:40,680 --> 00:38:43,160
such as extra-axial hematomas

516
00:38:43,160 --> 00:38:44,640
in the setting of trauma.

517
00:38:47,790 --> 00:38:49,390
Remember that as we evaluate

518
00:38:49,390 --> 00:38:51,410
each of these entities,

519
00:38:51,410 --> 00:38:53,470
we want to use the appropriate windows.

520
00:38:53,970 --> 00:38:55,630
So we're going to use the stroke windows

521
00:38:55,630 --> 00:38:57,210
when we're assessing the parenchyma,

522
00:38:57,370 --> 00:38:58,530
particularly for the gray-white matter

523
00:38:58,530 --> 00:38:59,050
differentiation.

524
00:38:59,810 --> 00:39:01,750
We might use the blood windows

525
00:39:01,750 --> 00:39:02,650
when trying to assess

526
00:39:02,650 --> 00:39:04,230
for acute intracranial hemorrhage.

527
00:39:04,650 --> 00:39:05,910
We're going to use bone windows

528
00:39:05,910 --> 00:39:06,990
to assess the bones

529
00:39:06,990 --> 00:39:08,170
and paranasal sinuses

530
00:39:08,170 --> 00:39:09,890
and a soft tissue window

531
00:39:09,890 --> 00:39:12,750
to assess the soft tissues

532
00:39:12,750 --> 00:39:13,590
and orbits.

533
00:39:15,090 --> 00:39:16,230
As we've gone through

534
00:39:16,230 --> 00:39:17,230
the search pattern,

535
00:39:17,490 --> 00:39:19,110
I've tried to hit the highlights

536
00:39:19,110 --> 00:39:20,950
of some basic neuroimaging concepts

537
00:39:20,950 --> 00:39:22,270
that you'll need

538
00:39:22,270 --> 00:39:23,450
to interpret a head CT.

539
00:39:24,150 --> 00:39:25,350
So we talked with assessment

540
00:39:25,350 --> 00:39:26,450
of the ventricles.

541
00:39:26,970 --> 00:39:28,770
The ventricles can either be too small.

542
00:39:29,070 --> 00:39:30,750
We saw cases of over-shunting

543
00:39:30,750 --> 00:39:32,610
and diffuse cerebral edema

544
00:39:32,610 --> 00:39:35,250
where the ventricles can be too large

545
00:39:35,250 --> 00:39:37,410
where the primary differential diagnosis

546
00:39:37,410 --> 00:39:39,310
is atrophy or hydrocephalus.

547
00:39:39,890 --> 00:39:41,450
We talked about the different types

548
00:39:41,450 --> 00:39:42,790
of hydrocephalus,

549
00:39:42,850 --> 00:39:44,590
which are essentially all obstructive.

550
00:39:45,170 --> 00:39:46,630
However, they are categorized

551
00:39:46,630 --> 00:39:47,990
on the level of obstruction,

552
00:39:48,150 --> 00:39:48,510
that is,

553
00:39:48,830 --> 00:39:49,930
is the obstruction

554
00:39:49,930 --> 00:39:52,070
intraventricular or extraventricular.

555
00:39:53,750 --> 00:39:54,970
Finally, we talked about

556
00:39:54,970 --> 00:39:56,610
the location of masses

557
00:39:56,610 --> 00:39:57,630
in fluid collections,

558
00:39:58,050 --> 00:39:59,430
whether they are intraaxial,

559
00:39:59,530 --> 00:40:00,970
which is within the brain substance,

560
00:40:01,210 --> 00:40:02,430
or extraaxial,

561
00:40:02,530 --> 00:40:04,090
that is outside the brain substance.

562
00:40:04,090 --> 00:40:05,870
This will be very important

563
00:40:05,870 --> 00:40:07,590
when trying to come up

564
00:40:07,590 --> 00:40:08,930
with a differential diagnosis

565
00:40:08,930 --> 00:40:10,590
for intracranial neoplasms.

566
00:40:11,810 --> 00:40:13,250
Then we went into discussion

567
00:40:13,250 --> 00:40:15,510
of the brain parenchyma

568
00:40:15,510 --> 00:40:17,550
and assessing patterns of edema.

569
00:40:18,050 --> 00:40:19,590
Remember, vasogenic edema

570
00:40:19,590 --> 00:40:21,590
from cellular ischemia and swelling

571
00:40:21,590 --> 00:40:24,070
results in loss

572
00:40:24,070 --> 00:40:25,630
of gray-white matter differentiation,

573
00:40:26,470 --> 00:40:27,770
whereas cytotoxic edema

574
00:40:27,770 --> 00:40:29,490
is secondary to leaky capillaries,

575
00:40:29,610 --> 00:40:30,590
typically associated

576
00:40:30,590 --> 00:40:32,130
with neoplasm or abscess,

577
00:40:32,130 --> 00:40:33,730
where that gray-white matter

578
00:40:33,730 --> 00:40:35,310
differentiation is maintained.

579
00:40:36,310 --> 00:40:37,190
And then finally,

580
00:40:37,350 --> 00:40:38,510
probably the most important thing

581
00:40:38,510 --> 00:40:39,390
we could have talked about

582
00:40:39,390 --> 00:40:41,450
is identifying the different types

583
00:40:41,450 --> 00:40:42,690
of herniation

584
00:40:42,690 --> 00:40:44,410
and realizing that

585
00:40:44,410 --> 00:40:45,710
these are emergent findings,

586
00:40:45,830 --> 00:40:46,530
so this is something

587
00:40:46,530 --> 00:40:48,310
that needs to be rapidly communicated

588
00:40:48,310 --> 00:40:50,130
with the referring service.

589
00:40:51,410 --> 00:40:52,450
That brings us

590
00:40:52,450 --> 00:40:53,630
to the end of this unit.

591
